<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Genesis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of Genesis is the first book of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and the Christian Old Testament, recounting the creation of the world, the Flood and the stories of Abraham, Isaac, Jacob and Joseph. It states that Noah's ark came to rest on the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Ararat</a>, a name derived from Urartu. Traditionally ascribed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, it was compiled from several sources and probably reached its final form in the sixth or fifth century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis.jpg" alt="Book of Genesis" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julius Schnorr von Carolsfeld · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schnorr_von_Carolsfeld_Bibel_in_Bildern_1860_001.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s name survives in the biblical Ararat, where in the <b>Book of Genesis</b> Noah’s ark came to rest; the modern Mount Ararat received that name much later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Book of Genesis</b> Sidon is the first-born son of Canaan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A thick layer of clay over the Ubaid levels was read by the excavators of the 1920s as the Flood of the Epic of Gilgamesh and the <b>Book of Genesis</b>, though later carbon dating gave such layers at different Sumerian cities different dates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In late antiquity the pyramids came to be taken for the granaries of Joseph from the <b>Book of Genesis</b>; the pilgrim Egeria recorded the idea on her visit between 381 and 384.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>It retells the story of Jacob's dream in the <b>Book of Genesis</b> (28:11–12), in which Jacob, sleeping with a stone for a pillow, sees a ladder reaching from earth to heaven with the angels of God going up and down on it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nearer-my-god-to-thee">Nearer, My God, to Thee</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Iron Age it was a Phoenician town whose people, the Zemarites, are listed in <b>Genesis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/simyra">Simyra</a></li></ul></section>
